WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.510
So, the previous module we built you a

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.190
design system that you can use for the

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.950
front end of your websites. In this

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.310
second part, we're now going to take

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.670
that design that you just built, which

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.510
is currently living in your own

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.230
computer, in your local device, and push

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.910
it out to the web so that people can

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.870
access it. And that takes us to phase

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.630
two of the Craft framework, which is

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.750
rigging or connecting the back end of

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.470
our website. And when we talk about the

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.430
back end, that basically means the core

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.430
functionality of whatever it is that

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.230
you're building. Similar to if you think

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.310
about building up a restaurant, the

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.110
front end of the restaurant, which is

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.990
also called the front of house, that is

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.630
what you design. That is what is

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.070
customer facing. But the back room, the

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.270
back of the house, is the back end. That

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.190
is the kitchen, and that is where the

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.150
cooking is taking place. And so for any

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.030
piece of software that you build,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.670
whether it's a website, a piece of

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.710
automation, or any system, you need to

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.670
consider that the back end works so that

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.830
the core functionality of the website

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.350
exists. Now with a build like this,

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.950
which is basically a landing page for

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.550
your agency website, the core

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.470
functionality is pretty simple, right?

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.070
Because your intention here is to just

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.950
have a professional website, that when

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.470
people land here and are actually

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.229
interested to talk to you, they can

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:06.870
actually do so. So what that means

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.190
specifically is if someone clicks on

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.870
book a call and they actually fill up

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.910
this form, then this email should send

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.190
to the right address that you declare.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.390
Now since this whole thing is just a

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.950
front end design and it's not really

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.070
rigged to anything yet, if you fill this

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.670
up and click on let's talk, it's not

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.510
really going to do anything because you

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.230
need to set up the back end first. And

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.030
this is going to generally be the same

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.910
process whether you're making a complex

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.310
software build or you're making

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.350
something simple like a website. Because

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.070
what I would do now in this case is I

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.510
would just ask Claude, "Hey, so I

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.470
actually want to set up the contact form

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.390
so that anyone who fills in the contact

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.230
form and clicks on let's talk, that

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.630
message actually gets sent to our email

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.510
at j@robonuggets.com.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.110
Can you do a bit of research and provide

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.310
me three options that we can use to set

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.950
this up?" And so with that, essentially

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.310
what we're doing is we're giving it the

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.110
core functionality of what we want to

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.670
happen. And right now Now, just planning

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.790
and asking it to give us options, and

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.510
since Claude and other agentic platforms

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.230
are really good at giving you options

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.750
for things, similar to how we planned

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.790
for the design of our front end earlier,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:05.990
we're now just offloading that to

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.710
Claude, so that we can decide how I'll

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.150
wire this up for a service that will let

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.830
us send emails to our desired address.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.310
And from that, it will give you a few

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:15.949
options, and this is generally the

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:17.949
process if in case you don't know yet

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:19.990
what solution or what service you need

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.150
to wire up to your website or your

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.630
software. But from here, you can see

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.430
that it also recommended we send, which

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.949
is actually what we will be connecting

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:28.630
to this build, so that when people fill

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.390
up this form, you will actually receive

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.510
the emails for real. So, if you go to

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.830
resend.com and sign up there, the way

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.750
you connect this application is via an

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.310
API key. So, you just need to head to

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.949
the API keys here, create one, and give

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:43.670
it a name, let's say for Agency X, give

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.790
it full permission, click on add, and if

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.590
I copy this in, send that to Claude, and

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.630
just say set up for me, here's the API

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.870
key, then you'll be able to connect

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.710
Resend to your Agency X website. should

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.310
now link up that form, so that your

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.030
email is connected there as well. So,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.630
now if we go to the contact form and

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.150
actually fill this up for real, and

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.310
let's say hello world, click on let's

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.430
talk, this button should now work, and

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.110
it says, "Thanks, your message is in.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.790
We'll be in touch." And if I now go to

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.630
that email address, you should see that

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.870
that email has been sent by

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.949
onboarding@resend.dev.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:16.630
Now, once you have created the back end,

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.310
it's always a good practice for you to

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.150
audit the work of your agent. And so,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.949
that brings us to step three of the

00:03:21.959 --> 00:03:24.390
Craft Framework, where we'll have Claude

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.110
do the test itself, so that it will

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.949
actually go to your website, click on

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:29.710
the buttons, submit the form, and just

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.990
double-check that everything works

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.270
before we ship it. And to do that, I'll

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.910
just say, "Hey, I want you to audit and

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.110
test this website. Just make sure that

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.509
all of the buttons and all of the

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:41.229
interactive elements are actually

00:03:41.239 --> 00:03:44.110
clickable and work as intended, and also

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.830
all of the functionality that we set up,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.150
such as sending an email through that

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.190
form at the bottom. Can you open the

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.070
browser on your side, and actually for

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.190
this demo, just show me the browser live

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.230
so that I can show my audience as well.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:57.350
And I'll just send that prompt. And

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.830
obviously that last part where I'm

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.510
opening the browser live, you don't

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:01.830
really have to do that, but I just

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:02.990
wanted to show you how Claude can do

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.510
this live as well, so that you can

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.190
follow along. And you can see here that

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.110
it's now invoking this tool called

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:09.949
Playwright. And Playwright is this free

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:11.510
tool, it's been around for a while, that

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.550
basically lets your AI agent control

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.230
your browser for things like testing,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.150
scripting, and pretty much anything that

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.910
you want to do in a browser. So, similar

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:20.509
to other applications, the way to

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:23.030
connect to it is via the CLI or an MCP

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.310
server, and all of those resources, you

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.070
can just grab the prompt below, and

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.550
you'll be able to set it up on your

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.110
machine as well. And I'll just let this

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.110
run for a while, and here you can see

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.430
it's now opening the browser, and it is

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.750
actually testing out all of those

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.270
elements. So, let's just let that run as

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:45.150
it goes along.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.510
There you go, you saw that it went

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.190
through the website, it actually tested

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.830
all of the interactive elements here,

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.590
and it's pretty much verified that all

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:52.710
of the buttons work. So, that's

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.190
something that you can do with each and

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.830
every build, which saves you a lot of

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.430
time instead of you having to do it

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:58.750
yourself. And back in our session, you

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.510
can see it gave us a summary of its

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.950
findings, and anything that it needs to

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.790
correct. So, there you go, that is now

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.110
how you introduce functionality to your

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:08.550
software build. First, you need to

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:09.590
identify what are the core

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.870
functionalities, and identify what

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.110
basically your app needs to be doing, or

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.790
your website needs to be doing. Then you

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.190
wire it up to any service that is

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.110
necessary to accomplish that function,

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.430
and then you do a simple self-audit,

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.430
which can be handled by your agent, so

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.830
that you can make sure that each piece

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.390
of functionality works before we deploy

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.720
it.
